<worksheet>
<title>Factoring by Grouping</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
    <p>
    In this section you will see how to:
    </p>
</introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Determine the greatest common factor of an expression.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor out the greatest common factor from an expression.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor an expression with an even number of terms by grouping.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    <term>Factoring</term> is the reverse process to multiplication. Recall that when you multiply with variables, you multiply the numbers and
    variables separately. When you multiply the variables, you add the exponents. For example <m>(4x^2)(2x^3) = 8x^5</m>. Factoring reverses
    the process so <m>8x^5 = (4x^2)(2x^3)</m>. 
</p>
<p>
    For polynomials we are interested in finding and factoring out the most we can from each term. This is called the 
    <term>Greatest Common Factor</term> or <alert>GCF</alert> for short. When dealing with variables, this means the GCF is the 
    common variable with the smallest exponent among all of the terms. For numbers, this means that if you factor each number, 
    the GCF is largest combination of factors from each term. For example, in the polynomial <m>8x^5 + 20x^4 + 28x^3</m>, the GCF
    is <m>4x^3</m> since the smallest exponent is 3 and when you factor the numbers, 8 = (4)(2), 20 = (4)(5) and 28 = (4)(7), 4 is the largest
    factor that is common to each term.
</p>
<example>
    <title>Identify the GCF</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            What is the GCF of the expression <m>30x^2 + 18x^2</m>?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>6x^2</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Since the lowest power of each term is <m>x^2</m>, 
            that is the GCF of the variable part. The GCF of 30 and 18 is 6,
            since <m>30 = 6\cdot 5</m> and <m>18=6\ cdot 3</m>. So the GCF is 
            <m>6x^2</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example>
<title>Identify the GCF</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        What is the GCF of the expression <m>28x^5+8x^4 + 12x^3</m>?
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>4x^3</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        The GCF of the variable part is <m>x^3</m> and GCF of the number part is 4. So the GCF is <m>4x^3</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<p>
<alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            What is the GCF of the following polynomial expressions?
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>16x^3-2x^2+12x</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>20x^5+8x^3+12x^2</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>25x^6 + 5x^4 + 15x^3</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
<p>
   Once you identify the GCF, you want to factor this out of each term. What is remaining from each term forms the other factor.
</p>
<example>
    <title>Factor Out the GCF</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Factor the expression <m>10x^8 + 6x^7 + 22x^5</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>2x^5(5x^3 + 3x^2+11)</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            The GCF is <m>2x^5</m>. When you factor <m>2x^5</m> out of <m>10x^8</m>, you are left with <m>5x^3</m> since <m>(5x^3)(2x^5) = 10x^8</m>. 
            When you factor <m>2x^5</m> out of <m>6x^7</m>, you are left with <m>3x^2</m> since <m>(3x^2)(2x^5) = 6x^7</m>. When you factor <m>2x^5</m>
            out of <m>22x^5</m>, you are left with <m>11</m> since <m>(11)(2x^5) = 22x^5</m>. So the final factorization is <m>2x^5(5x^3 + 3x^2+11)</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Factor Out the GCF</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Factor the expression <m>8x^3-12x^2y+20xy^2</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>4x(2x^2-3xy+5y^2)</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The GCF is <m>4x</m>. There are no <m>y</m>'s in the first term, so <m>y</m> is not included in the GCF. The final factorization is
                <m>4x(2x^2-3xy+5y^2)</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
        <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
    </p>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Factor the following expressions by factoring out the GCF.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>28x^7+8x^6+44x^4</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>9m^2n-36mn-288n</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>8x(5x-4y)-3(5x-4y)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <p>
        We can go one step further with factoring if we start with an even number of terms. This technique is called factoring by <term>grouping</term>. 
        We need and even number of terms so that we can form equal sized groups.
    </p>
    <example>
        <title>Factor by Grouping</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Factor the expression <m>xy+8y+3x+24</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>(x+8)(y+3)</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Split the expression into two groups <m>(xy+8y)</m> and <m>(3x+24)</m>. Factor the GCF out of each group: <m>y(x+8)</m> and <m>3(x+8)</m>. The complete 
                expression is now <m>y(x+8)+3(x+8)</m>. Notice that the factors in the parentheses match, so <m>(x+8)</m> is also a GCF for our now two terms. Factor this 
                out and the parts in front of the <m>(x+8)</m>'s form the other factor. The final factorization is <m>(x+8)(y+3)</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Factor by Grouping</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Factor the expression <m>4x^2-16x+3x-12</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>(x-4)(4x+3)</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>4x^2-16x+3x-12 \rightarrow 4x(x-4) + 3(x-4) \rightarrow (x-4)(4x+3)</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
        <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
    </p>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Completely factor the following expressions by grouping.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x^2+7x-3x-21</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x^2y-xy^2+2x-2y</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>w^2+2w+3w+6</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>